<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Šubić family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Šubići of Bribir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Šubić of Bribir were a Croatian noble family from Bribir in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, one of the twelve noble tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. They rose to great power in the late 13th century under Paul I Šubić, ban of Croatia and lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and dominated Croatia and Dalmatia until the fall of Mladen II in 1322. A later branch became the Zrinski family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/subic-family.jpg" alt="Šubić family" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en.wikipedia.org · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:643px-Bribir6_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family took its name from the county of Bribir in inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, known in the Roman period as Varvaria, the centre of one of the fourteen Liburnian counties. Its hill, about fifteen kilometres northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a>, commanded the routes from the sea to the hinterland. The Byzantine emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus, writing in the 10th century, named Breberi among the counties the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> had organised. Documents of the time call the kindred nobles, counts or rulers of Breber, and its members styled themselves de Breberio; the surname Šubić was chosen by Croatian historians of the 19th century, to whom it sounded more Slavic than Breber.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-zvonimir">Demetrius Zvonimir</a> (1075–1089) Budez and Dominicus of the lineage held high court offices, and in 1251 King Bela confirmed the kindred in its possession of Bribir. In the 13th and 14th centuries its members were often counts of Split, Trogir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Skradin</a> and Omiš, rivals of the Kačić and Nelipić families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-subic-of-bribir">Paul I Šubić of Bribir</a> and his brothers controlled the maritime cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>; he championed the papal cause and helped place Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. After Paul died in 1312, his eldest son Mladen tried to keep the other Croatian clans in hand but gradually lost land, castles and towns.</p><p>A numerous kin stayed at Bribir: in 1324, 190 members of the clan came forward when the citizenship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> was conferred on them. Ottoman raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> followed in the 15th century, the clan warriors were defeated at the Battle of Krbavsko Polje in 1493, and by 1520 the castle of Breber was rubble. Its families resettled in the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, in Turopolje and in the Zagorje region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1347 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> gave Count Gregory and Count George, son and nephew of Ban Paul, the Zrin Castle in exchange for Ostrovica. Their branch, the Zrinski (1347–1703), reached its height with Count Nikola IV (1508–1566), the hero of Szigetvár, and Count Nikola VII (1620–1664). Another branch, loyal to Sigismund of Luxemburg against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a>, received the castle of Perna and as the Perényi family counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> until the 20th century; Gabriel Perényi and Bishop Francis Perényi fell at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> on 29 August 1526. The descendants of Ugrinus (died 1335), called Melith, gained vast estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Šubić family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
